  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "There were NNN people lined up in a queue. The people are numbered 111, 222, …\\ldots…, NNN but not necessarily in the order they are lined up.\n\nUnfortunately, some of them can't even understand where the front of the queue is. So, some people are looking towards the front of the queue and some people are looking towards the back of the queue.\n\nThe iii-th person reports AiA_iAi​ as the number of people ahead of him **in the direction** he is looking.\n\nNow, you are wondering how many possible orders of people could be there in the queue. 222 orders are said to be different if at least 111 position iii, such that the iii-th person from the front of the queue are different.\n\nSince the answer may be large, output it modulo 998244353998244353998244353. The answer may also be 000, i.e. no valid order exists.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of multiple lines of input.\n\nThe first line contains NNN - the number of people in the queue.\nThe second line contains NNN integers - A1,A2,...,ANA_1, A_2, ..., A_NA1​,A2​,...,AN​.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the number of possible orders modulo 998244353998244353998244353.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1041 \\le T \\le 10^41≤T≤104",
      "1≤N≤2⋅1051 \\le N \\le 2 \\cdot 10^51≤N≤2⋅105",
      "0≤Ai&lt;N0 \\le A_i \\lt N0≤Ai​&lt;N",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 2⋅1052 \\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n2\n0 0\n3\n0 1 2\n3\n1 1 2",
        "output": "2\n2\n0",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest Case 1 : Both [1,2][1, 2][1,2] and [2,1][2, 1][2,1] are valid orders. For example, in the order [1,2][1, 2][1,2] it is possible the first person looks towards the front, and the second person looks towards the back. This way, both will count 000 people ahead of them in the direction they are facing.\nTest Case 2 : [1,2,3][1, 2, 3][1,2,3] and [3,2,1][3, 2, 1][3,2,1] are possible.\nTest Case 3 : No valid order exists."
      }
    ]
  },
